#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205188 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we’ll continue exploring the *total derivative* of a vector-valued function, which generalizes the derivative to functions with multiple inputs and outputs.\n\nSuppose a transformation $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by \n\n![](images/cd/cd9132d6a0e934349ae58a0718e6320cba3dd8ae84de7df2e3fb98b391256b57.svg) \n\nwhere $f_1,f_2,\\ldots,f_m$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables.\n\nIf $\\boldsymbol f$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf{x},$ then the **total derivative** of $\\boldsymbol f$ at $\\mathbf{x},$ denoted $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{x}),$ is given by the $m \\times n$ matrix\n\n![](images/c8/c8fbc309a129b8e19db46aebff637e47259b629ef83c282a38b3b318c3a4f269.svg)\n\nThis is sometimes called the **Jacobian matrix** of $\\boldsymbol{f}$ at $\\mathbf{x}$. Each row corresponds to the derivative of one component function $f_i$, and each column corresponds to the rate of change of all the components of $\\boldsymbol f$ with respect to one input variable $x_j$. This is sometimes called the **numerator layout** for derivatives because each row corresponds to each function component, which appears in the numerator positions of the partial derivatives.\n\nNotice that if $f: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is a scalar-valued function, then the total derivative is a row vector given by\n  ![](images/6e/6ef756e82eb71d528a8f279863fd86efc8669658456a65879fba73d08e964bd5.svg)\n\nOn the other hand, if $\\boldsymbol{f}: \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}^m$ is a vector-valued function of a single variable, then the total derivative is a column vector\n  ![](images/cd/cd232968d5ffff3040294f8093a42c91bccd3207f91bfc2f784cb53cad00441d.svg)\n\nNow let’s get some practice by working through a few examples of total derivatives." :tutorial/id #uuid "56f24ef3-119b-5459-90a4-c9d67357d755" :tutorial/math-academy-id 12299 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205189 :tutorial/content "We've learned that the derivative of a vector-valued function is a Jacobian matrix. Now imagine the input to our function $\\mathbf f$ is a matrix instead of a vector. In such cases, the Jacobian matrix must expand to account for two input dimensions.\n\nSuppose we have a transformation $\\boldsymbol{f} : \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n} \\to \\mathbb{R}^p$ that maps a matrix $\\mathbf{A} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n}$ to a vector $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{A}) \\in \\mathbb{R}^p$, defined as\n\n![](images/f7/f7b9ca5880f164c28b490fd4a215a56769e426bdde8111a24e6530c03d83b937.svg)\n\nwhere each $f_i$ is a scalar-valued function of all the entries of the matrix $\\mathbf{A}.$\n\nSince the input $\\mathbf{A}$ varies along two dimensions (rows and columns), and the output $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{A})$ varies along one (a vector with $p$ components), the total derivative of $\\mathbf f,$ which we denote as \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{f}}{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{A}}\n$$\n\nis naturally represented as a *three-dimensional object!* The technical name for this object is a **tensor**. Since it is a three-dimensional object, this particular tensor is a **third-order tensor** (or $3$-**tensor**).\n\nTo capture a meaningful derivative, we must differentiate each component $f_i$ with respect to *every element of the matrix* $\\mathbf A.$ We can visualize this as a stack of matrices, or layers, as shown below.\n\n![](images/d5/d533d4d2e387c8c9f177b9dafe939b58c1f1d7db2f03e9df2059fa9f7cbe22c5.png)\n\nLet's apply this concept to a specific example in the next slide." :tutorial/id #uuid "b89cedd8-d9d0-543f-82d2-8961bb1f4278" :tutorial/math-academy-id 12382 :tutorial/title "Tensor Derivatives"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205193 :tutorial/content "Let’s examine examples of functions defined by a matrix of constants multiplied by a vector more carefully.\n\nConsider the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}: \\mathbb{R}^{2} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^3$ defined by\n![](images/f8/f81cbd9b797d1f1b05edd5f254d6735456e937355fe0e6692a4a830da925351b.svg) \n\nSuppose we want to compute the total derivative $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{f}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}}$ at the point ![](images/cd/cd0b094634acde36ee93c2fb90c6f11ab56c2f675d27ba65077542d75cb451d6.svg).\n\nThe total derivative of a vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}: \\mathbb{R}^{n} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^m$ with respect to a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ is the $m \\times n$ matrix\n![](images/63/6305cfe857f2228d471b85f859302a43046470f1d182b34abbf3ab4b362e7e07.svg)\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{x})$ where ![](images/a8/a8d36d7195af007d90a4f62cf213e28c7f51ced97168fb16d31a2c9192799b53.svg), we get\n![](images/79/79110c30e4f827ccc401c0d8d9b8a08f35a88d12781d83c7e365171de0bbd67d.svg)\n\nFrom here, we have \n\n$$\nf_1(\\mathbf{x}) = 3x_1 + 0 \\cdot x_2, \\qquad f_2(\\mathbf{x}) = x_1 - x_2,\\qquad f_3(\\mathbf{x}) = 0 \\cdot x_1 + 2x_2.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the total derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is independent of $\\mathbf x,$ we also have\n\n![](images/c1/c187256b1ad2181f53482829d4d5076b0b09fc19f1d399aa41fb9da6ca0bc8f3.svg)\n\nIn general, if $\\mathbf f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f(\\mathbf x) = A\\mathbf x\n$$\n\nwhere $A\\in\\mathbb R^{m\\times n}$ is constant and $\\mathbf x\\in\\mathbb R^n,$ then\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{\\textrm d \\mathbf f}{\\textrm d \\mathbf x} = A}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9ece2ba6-41e2-5766-ad9c-96ea0a7180a0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 12446 :tutorial/title "Some Standard Derivatives"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205216 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have the function $\\mathbf f: \\mathbb R^{3\\times2} \\to \\mathbb R^{2},$ defined as\n\n![](images/8d/8d7f629f4b82087a1d23ef1253ce7eb98ca760310d92a2ac8cf8c3fd3a4d9473.svg)\n\nWe calculate the derivatives of $\\mathbf f$ layer by layer as follows:\n\n- **Layer 1**: The derivatives of the first component $f_1$ with respect to $\\mathbf A.$\n![](images/c4/c4dab9446396e82d20ebbb38e3aabce9908565aeb7aeb48f2804a271d6ddf66f.svg)\n\n- **Layer 2**: The derivatives of the second component $f_2$ with respect to $\\mathbf A.$\n![](images/ce/ce7e31c8c3faf75aa7980a0e4f5f707f84f116e97b30c8b0dbd504bcfb4822c8.svg)\n\nCombining these layers into a 3D block, we get the total derivative (a $3$-tensor in $\\mathbb R^{2\\times3\\times2}$):\n\n![](images/3d/3d6eeebb92218d4f0995dbccd74b444da112f0e55b22ade0bbaa0259e06e9bb0.png)\n\nYou've probably realized by now that working with 3-tensors directly is cumbersome! So, rather than trying to visualize the full tensor all at once, focusing on its components is often helpful. Generally,\n\n$$\n\\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{f}}{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{A}} \\right)_{i, j, k} = \\dfrac{\\partial f_i}{\\partial a_{jk}}\n$$\n\nis a *scalar* representing the derivative of the $i$th output component with respect to the element $a_{jk}$ of the matrix $\\mathbf A.$\n\nFor example, the derivative of $f_{\\color{red}1}$ with respect to $a_{{\\color{blue}3},{\\color{blue}2}}$ corresponds to the component at index $({\\color{red}1},{\\color{blue}3},{\\color{blue}2})$ of a tensor. In our example, we saw that\n![](images/4f/4fa3d21f5d62bd01491c49b3351cdd3cf751a7ad793509d1c2b3846e4f6825cd.svg)\n\nLooking at the ${\\color{red}1}$st layer (front face), row ${\\color{blue}3},$ column ${\\color{blue}2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d \\mathbf f}{\\textrm d \\mathbf A}\\right)_{{\\color{red}1}, {\\color{blue}3}, {\\color{blue}2}} = \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{0}.\n$$\n\nDerivatives of matrix functions arise in optimization problems, machine learning (e.g., backpropagation through layers), and physics (e.g., tensor fields). Understanding their structure is key to efficient computation and interpretation." :tutorial/id #uuid "4f4a75d3-35bf-59fd-adcb-45b912cba094" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14941 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"}]}
